  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 303 PM CDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nRain chances begin to wind down Thursday morning into the afternoon \nhours, as an upper level low pushes south of the region. While this \nmay keep rain chances continuing to the east, confidence lowers on \nwrap around precipitation continuing across our region. Worth \nremembering that the Flood Watch remains in effect through Thursday \nevening, as waters may take time to recede if flooding occurs. \n\nWith the upper level low remaining to the south, guidance continues \nto suggest the low will bring in a mass of cooler, Canadian air \nacross the region. This cooler air mass is expected to bring \ntemperatures below seasonal average, with highs remaining in the 60s \ninto the weekend. As a slight push of warmer air occurs on Saturday, \nmay briefly see highs reach the lower 70s, but for the most part, \nexpecting 60s into early next week. For reference, early October, we \ntypically see highs around 71 to 73 degrees with lows around 40 to \n42 degrees. So, even if we do see a slight temperature increase on \nSaturday, expecting it would mostly remain near climatological \nnormal. However, brace for the more seasonal low temperatures to \nreturn near the end of the week. We have mostly remained well above \nour seasonal normal lows in large part to the higher dewpoints \novernight. Thursday night into Friday morning, widespread lows are \nexpected to be in the low to mid 40s, and while not unusual for \nOctober, still about 10 to 15 degrees cooler than we have been \nexperiencing. For now, forecast trends indicate that we should be \nnear to slightly above seasonal with our lows through the weekend, \nwith lows in the mid to upper 40s. Again, while not unusual for \nOctober, folks with outdoor plants and animals sensitive to cooler \ntemperatures may want to pay attention to this change in the \npattern. Forecast trends continue to suggest that we may finally be \nseeing more frequent fall like weather late this week into next \nweek.",
